nodecode-brave-search is a rewrite of rabilrbl's rabilrbl/hermes-brave-search-plugin:

https://github.com/rabilrbl/hermes-brave-search-plugin
commit 4134cbef910c14c63e6b284b0820b30f3811ab47

MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Rabil (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the plugin's one tool, brave_web_search, is here a tool of
the same name and the same call as a Lisp verb, brave:search. It makes the same
request the Python did -- GET api.search.brave.com/res/v1/web/search, the
subscription token in X-Subscription-Token, the same six parameters (query,
count, country, search_lang, freshness, safesearch, count clamped to 1-20) --
and lifts the same fields out of the answer: title, url, description, age,
language, source and display_url, with the HTML tags and entities Brave sends
cleaned off the text. The requests dependency rides the image's own HTTP call,
and the key moves from an environment variable into the cell's `brave-search'
section, which still reads BRAVE_API_KEY when no key is pasted there.

What was left out: the JSON envelope itself. The original answered a JSON
object with a results array; this cell answers the same fields as one block a
result, which is what a model reads, and carries a failure as an error naming
Brave's own sentence instead of an "error" member. The original's check_fn
hid the tool when no key was set; here a section naming no key refuses the
start instead, so the tool is never offered without one. Nothing else of the
plugin is missing: it had no commands, hooks, skills or prompts beyond the
tool's schema, which the cell's contract carries member for member.
